WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.900
A legal gap in Canada Species at Risk Act leaves 153 of 288 cases overdue for protection as of January 2025. This is Environmental Intelligence Episode 72. The Species at Risk Act contains a loophole that delays protection for listed species. The Canadian Climate Institute states current federal and provincial climate initiatives fall short of required reductions. Practitioners should examine SARA listing timelines and habitat order implications for projects in affected regions this week.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:35.120
The documented backlog creates direct compliance uncertainty for assessments that rely on

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:35.940
published orders.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:41.140
Federal and provincial environmental assessment processes now operate against an incomplete

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.380
set of protection triggers.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.620
If you only note one thing today for your compliance calendar, verify current critical

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:51.960
habitat order status for any SARA listed species on active assessment files.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:57.960
A CBC investigation identifies a procedural gap in the Species at Risk Act that allows listed

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:03.060
species to remain without critical habitat orders or recovery strategies for extended periods.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:09.640
The study documents 153 unresolved cases out of 288 as of January 2025.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:15.140
Some species have waited multiple years after initial listing without further protection measures.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:20.040
This directly affects project screening under the Impact Assessment Act and Provincial

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:25.540
Environmental Assessment processes, where SARA listed species trigger additional review requirements.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.500
consultants conducting phase one and phase two assessments in jurisdictions with known

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:36.080
occurrences must verify current listing status and any pending habitat orders before submitting reports.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:42.460
The absence of a fixed timeline for ministerial decisions on protection creates uncertainty in permitting schedules.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.500
Federal provincial coordination on enforcement remains inconsistent across provinces.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:53.620
The procedural gap means listing alone does not activate the full suite of obligations practitioners expect.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:58.680
Project proponents therefore phase variable risk depending on whether a particular species

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.120
has reached the order stage.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:06.720
The study highlights that 153 cases remain open without resolution, leaving habitat protection

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.140
incomplete for those species.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:13.580
Practitioners must therefore treat every SARA listed species occurrence as potentially active

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.920
until the registry confirms the order status.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.340
Review any SARA listed species occurrences on current assessment sites given the documented

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:27.820
backlog of 153 cases. Monitor federal updates on carbon pricing adjustments that may affect

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:33.060
industrial emissions reporting obligations. Check provincial environmental assessment registries

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:38.700
for any new SARA-related guidance emerging from the identified legal gap. Confirm whether any

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:45.540
active files involve species listed before 2023 that still lack published orders. When a site

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:50.860
assessment identifies potential habitat for a listed species, confirm the most recent critical

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.540
Habitat order status through the federal species at risk public registry rather than relying

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:57.380
solely on the initial listing date.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:02.240
The backlog means some species have recovery strategies published while others with

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.160
identical listing dates do not, creating uneven compliance triggers.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:11.900
Cross reference with provincial wildlife databases because enforcement of habitat protection orders

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:15.480
varies by jurisdiction even when federal listing applies.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:22.280
Update internal checklists to flag any species with listing dates before 2023 that still lack

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:27.720
published orders. The verification step prevents later regulator requests for additional surveys

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:33.200
after submission. Consultants must now treat listing dates as unreliable indicators of when

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.920
protection requirements activate. A phase two report flags potential habitat for a species listed

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:44.900
three years ago, yet the federal registry shows no critical habitat order published. The experience

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.160
Once practitioner checks the registry date first rather than assuming the listing automatically

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.120
triggers review.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:55.620
The science here is that SARA protection obligations attach only when a critical habitat order

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:59.720
or recovery strategy is actually enforced, not at the moment of listing.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.920
Field teams sometimes submit assessments citing the listing date alone and receive regulator

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.260
requests for additional surveys months later.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.780
The common mistake is treating the initial listing notice as the compliance trigger.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:16.880
The fix is to add a registry checkstep to every species screening protocol and document

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.860
the order publication date in the report.

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:24.160
Review the species at risk public registry for any listed species on current or upcoming

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:25.220
assessment sites.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:30.020
Update internal Sarah screening checklists to include verification of critical habitat

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.440
order status rather than listing data loan.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:38.740
Brief project teams on the 153-case backlog and its effect on permitting schedule assumptions.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:43.560
Monitor federal and provincial environmental assessment registries for any new guidance

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:45.940
responding to the identified procedural gap.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:51.000
Track any federal updates addressing the species at risk act protection timeline gap.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:56.260
Watch for provincial responses to the Canadian Climate Institute assessment of current reduction

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:56.660
measures.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:59.200
That's environmental intelligence for today.

